  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1037 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThis Afternoon: Storm Details\n\nSemi-discrete elevated convection developed over eastern South \nDakota early this morning on the nose of a 60-70-kt upper level jet \nstreak. The steering flow advected these storms off the narrow \ninstability axis by mid morning, but new convection has already \nstarted to percolate in their immediate wake on the nose of the 850-\n700-mb theta-e ridge nose ahead of the synoptic cold front that is \nnearly bifurcating Minnesota from SW to NE. Given the linear forcing \nand compact hodographs, these storms are expected to merge into a \nconvective complex and then turn southeast as the cold pools become \nmore established. The main question at this stage is how far east \nthese storms advance before turning south. Isolated corridors of 1\" \nhail are possible with the incipient discrete updrafts before the \nthreat transitions to damaging winds as the cells merge and grow \nupscale. Pockets of heavy rain and flooding could also take place \nwith any of the cells, especially before the complex starts \npropagating southeastward.\n\nThe latest rounds of the HRRR are at least trying to latch onto the \nlatest convective trends with this secondary line of storms, albeit \ndelayed 2-3 hours from the current time. Using this guidance as a \nproxy, the southeastward turn would take place west of the \nMississippi River by early afternoon as the cold front \napproaches, with the possibility of the line partially filling \nin with new convection by mid-afternoon--to what degree is less \ncertain. The storm threat will cease once the front passes by \nthis evening.\n\n\nSaturday Night - Friday: Periodic Storm Chances\n\nQuasi-NW/zonal flow remains the dominant flow pattern in both \nensemble and deterministic guidance over the coming week. With the \ncold front passage today, this will shift storm chances further to \nour south for Saturday. By Saturday night a low forms in the Central \nPlains which in turn shifts the baroclinic zone back north over \nportions of our forecast area for both Sunday and Monday. There \nstill remains quite a bit of uncertainty regarding how storms will \nform and where exactly they form. The GFS has the boundary further \ninto our area compared to the CMC and ECMWF which keep it further \ninto central Iowa for Saturday night then bring it back north for \nSunday into Monday. Given that we will be in the aforementioned \nnorthwest flow pattern, any perturbation could be enough to cause \nstorms to form whether along a boundary or become a MCS and move \nthrough the area. These mesoscale details are tough to pin down this \nfar out and are directly impacted by the previous day's convection. \n\nBy midweek, guidance has been in decent agreement that this boundary \nshifts south of the local area keeping the majority of the \nprecipitation chances south of the CWA. Similar to the last few \ndiscussions, with good agreement on the flow pattern and daily storm \nchances, high temperatures are likely to stay in the normal to \nslightly below normal range with highs in the mid 70s to mid\n80s.",
